WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:05.120
As we previously discussed, the Unreal Engine's reflection system does some of the memory

2
00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.560
management for us, however we should have a deeper dive into the garbage collector.

3
00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:14.660
As mentioned earlier, data is marked for memory management using the reflection macros.

4
00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:19.339
For instance, for a member variable to be managed, it must be marked up with UProperty.

5
00:00:19.339 --> 00:00:23.539
Unreal keeps a root set of UObjects, acting as a base for the garbage collector's

6
00:00:23.539 --> 00:00:27.989
iterative searches, and we'll touch on how it uses that root set in just a second.

7
00:00:27.989 --> 00:00:32.289
Many objects can be added to the root set, and actors can be explicitly destroyed, removing

8
00:00:32.289 --> 00:00:35.350
them from the root set and allowing them to be GC'd.

9
00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:38.869
This will also mean that their components can be GC'd as well, as their link to that

10
00:00:38.869 --> 00:00:40.869
root set has been broken.

11
00:00:40.869 --> 00:00:46.549
Once GC starts, Unreal proceeds through the following steps to find what needs collecting.

12
00:00:46.549 --> 00:00:50.329
We can see in Figure 1, when the garbage collector kicks in, all UObjects are marked

13
00:00:50.329 --> 00:00:53.130
as unreachable from the root set.

14
00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:56.710
At this point, all objects are considered viable for garbage collection.

15
00:00:56.710 --> 00:01:01.700
We need to process all references to see if anything truly is unreachable.

16
00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:06.219
We can see in Figure 2, Unreal builds a reference tree, breaking down how one object is linked

17
00:01:06.219 --> 00:01:07.519
to another.

18
00:01:07.519 --> 00:01:11.340
We can see that we've got a single tree from the root set with two branches.

19
00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:15.099
In this case, we can see that nothing is left truly unreachable, and none of the

20
00:01:15.099 --> 00:01:17.280
objects will be viable for garbage collection.

21
00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:21.340
If the UObject is still unreachable once we've iterated over all of the objects, we can

22
00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:24.250
commence collecting that object.

23
00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:28.010
Moving on to Figure 3, we can see that if we were to break the reference between Object

24
00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:32.969
1 and Object 3, we can see that the Object 3 to Object 4 branch is now unreachable in

25
00:01:32.969 --> 00:01:37.310
its entirety, and will be appropriately garbage collected.

26
00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:41.349
We discussed earlier, when talking about reflection macros, how the use of UClass gives

27
00:01:41.349 --> 00:01:46.629
us access to all UFunction and UProperty specifiers, allowing Unreal to manage the memory for

28
00:01:46.629 --> 00:01:47.719
us.

29
00:01:47.719 --> 00:01:53.090
However, we may want to keep a reference to a reflected type inside of a regular class.

30
00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:57.150
As that class isn't a reflected type itself, it will not show up in that initial reference

31
00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:00.780
tree, and any members inside will be unreachable.

32
00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:05.319
We can solve this by extending our class from the FGCObject class, and implementing

33
00:02:05.319 --> 00:02:10.580
its AddReferencedObject or AddReferencedObjects functions.

34
00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:14.319
The members added to the FReferenceCollector will be managed as if they have been marked

35
00:02:14.319 --> 00:02:16.849
up by a UProperty specifier.

36
00:02:16.849 --> 00:02:23.669
As long as the GCObject remains in existence, the UObjects within it will remain referenced.

37
00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:26.849
When working with the garbage collector as engineers and developers, there's a few things

38
00:02:26.849 --> 00:02:29.240
we really should keep in mind.

39
00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:31.879
UObject manipulation isn't thread safe.

40
00:02:31.879 --> 00:02:35.580
It's highly recommended that UObjects are accessed from the game thread.

41
00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:39.719
In cases where we want access on other threads, proper use of synchronization primitives

42
00:02:39.719 --> 00:02:43.900
are required to avoid common parallel programming errors, and be cautious as this could

43
00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:46.569
result in a loss of performance.

44
00:02:46.569 --> 00:02:50.969
The garbage collector cannot see raw pointers inside UObjects, so always use reflective

45
00:02:50.969 --> 00:02:54.139
properties or smart pointers when possible.

46
00:02:54.139 --> 00:02:58.099
An object can be marked as pending kill, meaning the developer wants the object to

47
00:02:58.099 --> 00:03:00.919
be garbage collected even if it is still referenced.

48
00:03:00.919 --> 00:03:02.900
This will not cause the immediate destruction.

49
00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:05.900
It merely indicates to the garbage collector that this object should be considered for

50
00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:09.909
cleanup next time the garbage collector is triggered.

51
00:03:09.909 --> 00:03:14.090
The Unreal Engine offers C++ programmers a smart pointer library, which is Epic's

52
00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:17.930
own flavor of C++ smart pointers.

53
00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:22.110
It's important to know that Unreal smart pointers are for non-UObjects, because as

54
00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:26.349
we discussed in the previous slides, reflected types are handled by a different memory tracking

55
00:03:26.349 --> 00:03:29.780
system, with the exception of the TWig object pointer.

56
00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:35.620
These T-pointers and T-refs are platform-agnostic versions of the standard C++ smart pointers,

57
00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:40.169
and have been designed to behave similarly to their C++ counterparts.

58
00:03:40.169 --> 00:03:44.210
These pointers allow us to manage the lifespan of non-reflected types with knowledge

59
00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:48.280
that they'll be cleaned up safely when all the references have been removed.

60
00:03:48.319 --> 00:03:54.360
Aside from the C++-based smart pointers, we also have access to TWig object pointers.

61
00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:58.629
This smart pointer is unique in that it is purely for reflected types.

62
00:03:58.629 --> 00:04:02.990
A TWig object pointer behaves exactly as a standard library's Wig pointer does.

63
00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:06.389
It will not keep the object alive or kept away from the garbage collector.

64
00:04:06.389 --> 00:04:11.349
A TWig object pointer can be assessed for validity without accessing the pointer itself,

65
00:04:11.349 --> 00:04:15.990
which is very, very useful when working with a lambda-heavy codebase.

66
00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:19.149
Smart pointers provide a number of benefits to the Unreal programmer.

67
00:04:19.149 --> 00:04:22.230
They help prevent memory leaks, as the memory is released as soon as the inner pointer is

68
00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:24.110
no longer referenced anywhere.

69
00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:27.310
They can be thread-safe, with the caveat that the thread-safe versions are slower

70
00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:31.709
than the non-thread-safe versions because of the atomic reference counting.

71
00:04:31.709 --> 00:04:35.350
And they also provide a great deal of runtime safety, in that a T-shared ref cannot

72
00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:39.269
be null, and it can be dereferenced safely.

73
00:04:39.269 --> 00:04:42.670
T-shared and the shared pointer inside the standard library are the same size under the

74
00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:43.670
hood.

75
00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:49.180
However, we'd opt for Unreal's flavor of shared pointer for its platform agnosticism.

76
00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:52.779
Objects don't intrinsically know that they're wrapped in a T-shared pointer, so Unreal

77
00:04:52.779 --> 00:04:56.680
presents a solution, the T-shared from this class.

78
00:04:56.680 --> 00:05:00.639
Inheriting from T-shared from this with a template allows us to grab a T-shared ref

79
00:05:00.639 --> 00:05:06.889
to the class using the following functions, the as-shared and shared this functions.

80
00:05:06.889 --> 00:05:10.290
It should be noted that whilst Unreal smart pointers are a fantastic tool for developers,

81
00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:12.029
they do not come for free.

82
00:05:12.029 --> 00:05:16.069
They do incur a small performance cost when passing them around, and as such it's advisable

83
00:05:16.069 --> 00:05:19.230
that the underlying pointer is passed around as a const ref instead.
